<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll">
  <p>Guy's and St Thomas' NHS Foundation Trust needed a new intranet. With over 23,000 staff across five hospitals and 23 community health centres, the scale of the research challenge was unlike anything a standard discovery process could handle. Night shift workers, porters, community health staff, people who would never make it into a research session. How do you make sure those voices shape what gets built?</p>
  <p>That question led somewhere interesting.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="gstt-what">
  <h2 id="gstt-what">What I did</h2>
  <p>I led a mixed-methods discovery across two phases.</p>
  <p>In the first phase, I ran 61 interviews combining guerrilla research, approaching staff directly in the canteen at St Thomas', with longer remote sessions. Participants spanned clinical, administrative, community and leadership roles across both GSTT and Royal Brompton and Harefield sites.</p>
  <p>In the second phase, I introduced Sonar, an AI-moderated interview tool I co-developed with Torchbox, to reach the staff our traditional methods couldn't. The asynchronous format meant someone could complete an interview in a break room on their phone, between patients, or at midnight after a shift. 85 more staff participated this way, including porters, matrons, shift workers and community health roles that had been almost impossible to recruit through conventional means.</p>
  <p>The two phases were analysed together, with the AI data tested against the human interviews for consistency and used to extend findings into areas we hadn't been able to reach.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="gstt-problem">
  <h2 id="gstt-problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>Staff described the intranet as essential and almost universally frustrating. Search was the most mentioned pain point, by a significant margin. People couldn't find things they knew existed. Policies sat alongside outdated versions with no clear way to tell which was current. Entire teams had stopped trusting the system and built their own informal workarounds: bookmarked links, shared drives, PDFs saved locally, phone calls to colleagues whose numbers weren't in the directory.</p>
  <p>The consequences weren't just inconvenient. A pharmacist described nurses calling her at 2am, not because they needed clinical expertise, but because they couldn't find something on the intranet that should have taken 30 seconds to locate.</p>
  <blockquote><p>I want the most recent and relevant thing to come up first, because the reality is, we need to get slicker. I don't have time to go searching for stuff.</p></blockquote>
  <p>Mobile and remote access was a particular barrier for community staff, who had to set up a personal hotspot, connect a work laptop and navigate VPN authentication just to check a policy between home visits.</p>
  <blockquote><p>To access the intranet out in the community, I have to get a hotspot on my work phone and connect my work laptop to the hotspot, and then go through Global Protect to work remotely and then access the intranet.</p></blockquote>
  <p>And some staff were using their personal phones on the ward to access clinical guidance, because there weren't enough computers. Which was leading to complaints from patients who thought nurses were scrolling social media.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="gstt-ai">
  <h2 id="gstt-ai">What the AI research added</h2>
  <p>The Sonar phase didn't just replicate what we already knew. It reached people we'd otherwise have missed entirely, and gave them a way to participate that fit around their working lives.</p>
  <p>It also surfaced things that hadn't come through in the human interviews. Staff with dyslexia asking for alternative content formats. Community health workers describing using national guidance instead of local guidance because the local version was simply impossible to find remotely. Matrons asking for wellbeing resources to be accessible from personal phones, because the moments when people most need support are rarely on a shift at a desktop.</p>
  <blockquote><p>It would be good if we could access wellbeing information from our personal phone, on our days off, if we're requiring any assistance with wellbeing. At the moment it's only available on GTI through the network.</p></blockquote>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="gstt-changed">
  <h2 id="gstt-changed">What changed</h2>
  <p>The discovery produced nine strategic recommendations covering search, content governance, navigation, mobile access, the staff directory, multi-site unification and the content editing experience. The findings are now shaping the build of a new intranet on Wagtail NHS Intranet, an open-source platform developed by Torchbox in collaboration with NHS partners.</p>
  <p>I presented the AI interview approach at the Local Government Technology Conference alongside Guy's and St Thomas' Product Manager for Digital Communications, to a strong reception. It has since been presented at a CEO charity leaders' breakfast and written up and shared widely as a model for large-scale qualitative research in the public sector.</p>
